          'rjust(), ljust() and center() pad a string to a given width with a fill character, which is how plain-text tables are aligned without a formatting library. strip() removes whitespace from both ends of a string, with lstrip() and rstrip() doing one end each; passing a string argument strips any of those characters rather than the whole string as a unit, which surprises people who expect it to remove a prefix. None of these methods modifies the original, so the result must be assigned. pyperclip is a third-party module that reads and writes the system clipboard with copy() and paste(), turning a script into something that slots into whatever the user is already doing.',
        takeaway: "strip('abc') removes any of a, b or c from the ends — it is a character set, not a prefix.",
